WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
We wanted to know whether making insulin more affordable could improve diabetes care,

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.820
so we studied nearly five million Medicare beneficiaries with type two diabetes

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:16.642
as the thirty-five-dollar monthly cap rolled out in twenty twenty-one and expanded in twenty twenty-three.

4
00:00:16.642 --> 00:00:22.385
After the expansion, insulin users paid about fifty-nine dollars less out of pocket per quarter

5
00:00:22.385 --> 00:00:24.694
and filled slightly more insulin.

6
00:00:24.694 --> 00:00:29.030
Among patients with lab results, average blood sugar also improved modestly,

7
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:35.033
although serious low-blood-sugar events became slightly more common across the study population.

8
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:44.552
Our findings suggest that lower costs can improve access, while persistent costs for other medicines point to the need for broader affordability policies.

9
00:00:44.552 --> 00:00:47.647
You can read our full study in JAMA Internal Medicine.
